WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 289: Alfred the Great: The Burnt Cakes of Athelney, the Danelaw, and the Defense of Wessex

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.360
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.560
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:12.960
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series honors the only English sovereign ever accorded the title, The Great.

4
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.440
[Rebecca] The pious scholar king who saved the English nation from Viking conquest.

5
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:27.200
[Rebecca] As we explore his dramatic life, we will examine the famous folklore of the burnt cakes, acknowledging it as legend while celebrating the true genius of his leadership.

6
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:33.840
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of King Alfred the Great.

7
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:44.560
[George] Born in 849 at the royal estate of Wantage in Berkshire, Alfred was the fifth and youngest son of King Ethelwolf of Wessex and his noble wife Osbar.

8
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:55.600
[George] Frail in physical constitution and plagued throughout his life by painful, mysterious gastrointestinal illnesses, young Alfred exhibited a brilliant, scholarly intellect.

9
00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:10.240
[George] When his mother showed her young sons a beautifully illuminated manuscript of Saxon poetry, promising it to the first boy who could recite it from memory, young Alfred found a tutor, memorized the verses overnight, and claimed the prize.

10
00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:20.800
[George] At age four and again at seven, Alfred made the perilous pilgrimage across the Alps to Rome, where Pope Leo IV confirmed him and received him as an adoptive son.

11
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:34.560
[George] Witnessing the majesty of Roman law, classical architecture, and Latin scholarship, Alfred was indelibly marked by the conviction that Christian learning, literacy, and divine order were the true bedrock of civil society.

12
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:48.720
[George] Growing up in the rural palace at Wantage, young Alfred was immersed in the oral traditions of the West Saxon Skop, the bard who recited heroic tales of Beowulf and ancient Germanic kings around peat burning hearths at winter feasts.

13
00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:54.400
[George] His visits to the papal court of Rome were an extraordinary education for an impressionable boy.

14
00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:01.080
[George] Walking through the colossal marble ruins of the Roman Forum and praying in the ancient basilicas of Constantine.

15
00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:12.360
[George] Alfred saw firsthand that enduring civilizational power rested on written administrative law, scholarly libraries, and universal literacy rather than brute physical force alone.

16
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:26.200
[George] His chronic digestive disease, which caused him sudden, agonizing abdominal spasms without warning, forced him to develop an extraordinary inner stoicism and intellectual discipline that sustained him through future crises.

17
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:31.720
[George] In 865, the existential crisis of Anglo-Saxon England arrived.

18
00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:40.760
[George] The great heathen army, led by the sons of Ragnar Lodbrok, Ivar the Boneless, Hafden and Ubba, landed on the shores of East Anglia.

19
00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:48.600
[George] Unlike previous coastal raiders who looted monasteries and sailed away, this massive Danish force came to conquer and settle.

20
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:56.440
[George] Within five years, Northumbria, Mercia, and East Anglia were completely shattered, their Christian kings martyred or exiled.

21
00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.120
[George] Only Wessex stood.

22
00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:06.280
[George] In the brutal year of battles in 871, 21-year-old Alfred fought alongside his older brother King Ethelred.

23
00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:16.600
[George] At the Battle of Ashdown, while Ethelred remained in his tent praying, Alfred seized command, charging like a wild boar up the chalk hills to root the Danish earls.

24
00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:27.080
[George] When Ethelred died of battle wounds weeks later, the Witon chose Alfred as king over Ethelred's infant sons, knowing that Wessex required a seasoned warrior.

25
00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.480
[George] The great heathen army was unlike any threat Western Europe had faced.

26
00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:43.960
[George] Landing with hundreds of clinkerbuilt dragonships, they seized horses from monasteries, moved with lightning tactical mobility, and established fortified winter camps that paralyzed Anglo Saxon resistance.

27
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:56.960
[George] At Ashdown in 871, when King Ethelred refused to leave his royal tent until the priest completed the Holy Mass, the twenty one-year-old Alfred demonstrated supreme military initiative.

28
00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:05.280
[George] Forming his thanes into a dense boar's snout formation and leading an uphill frontal charge through a solitary stunted thorn tree.

29
00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:18.800
[George] In five grueling pitched battles fought across Hampshire and Berkshire in just six months, Alfred learned the hard lesson that individual battlefield heroics could not permanently halt Danish migration without systemic structural reform.

30
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:31.680
[George] In early January 878, while Alfred and his court were celebrating the feast of Twelfth Night at Chippenham, the Danish warlord Guthrum launched a daring, unprecedented midwinter surprise attack.

31
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:41.760
[George] Overwhelmed and caught completely off guard, Alfred's defenses collapsed, and the king was forced to flee into the freezing wilderness with a handful of loyal thanes.

32
00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:55.680
[George] Taking refuge in the impassable tidal marshes of Somerset at the Isle of Athelney, surrounded by dense willow swamps, reeds and treacherous bogs, Alfred's kingdom had shrunk to a muddy fortress barely a few acres wide.

33
00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:03.280
[George] Wessex appeared utterly conquered, its people fleeing across the English Channel to France or submitting to Danish pagan rule.

34
00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.720
[George] From this miserable bog, the fate of the English speaking world hung by a single thread.

35
00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:21.360
[George] Guthrum's lightning winter raid on Chippenham was a psychological and military masterstroke that violated all traditional conventions of medieval warfare, which ceased military campaigns during the winter cold.

36
00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:37.880
[George] Driven into the sodden marshes of Somerset, where the river's tone and Parrot overflowed into tidal willow fens, Alfred and his tiny band of retainers were reduced to hunting wild boars, netting eels, and launching guerrilla ambushes against isolated Danish scouting parties.

37
00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:43.080
[George] The king's physical survival in the freezing winter damp was an act of raw endurance.

38
00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:56.200
[George] Sleeping in rough reed lews on earthen mounds, Alfred maintained royal authority through unyielding spiritual faith, sending secret envoys through the fens to rally the scattered West Saxon Eildormen.

39
00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:06.680
[George] It was during this desperate period of wilderness exile in Athelney that English folklore birthed its most famous royal legend, the story of Alfred and the burnt cakes.

40
00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:09.240
[George] According to the 12th century life of St.

41
00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:20.120
[George] Neote, an account preserved in popular memory rather than historical chronicle, the disguised king sought shelter in the crude wattle and daub hut of an impoverished swineherd.

42
00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:30.520
[George] The peasant's wife, unaware that her silent guest was the sovereign of Wessex, instructed him to watch the loaves of bread baking on the hearth embers while she attended to her chores.

43
00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:40.200
[George] Lost in dark contemplation over the ruin of his kingdom, his weapons and his people, Alfred neglected the fire, allowing the loaves to burn to cinders.

44
00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:45.240
[George] Returning to a smoke-filled hut, the furious peasant woman scolded him sharply.

45
00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:46.760
[George] You lazy rogue.

46
00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:52.280
[George] You won't turn the bread when you see it burning, but you're quite eager enough to eat it when it's done.

47
00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:59.640
[George] Rather than revealing his royal identity and pride, the chastened king humbly apologized and helped bake the next batch.

48
00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:05.400
[George] While historians recognize this tale as pious folklore, it captured a profound truth.

49
00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:11.960
[George] Alfred was a king who learned Christian humility, patience, and resilience in the crucible of adversity.

50
00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.640
[George] Historical scholars.

51
00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:30.000
[George] Analyzing the Vita Sancti Neodi where the Burnt Cakes episode first appeared three centuries after Alfred's death, recognize that the story belongs to the rich tradition of European royal folklore, akin to King C Nut commanding the waves or Robert the Bruce watching the spider.

52
00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:37.280
[George] Yet its enduring resonance in English cultural memory reflects a fundamental truth about Alfred's exceptional character.

53
00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:45.120
[George] Unlike continental monarchs who ruled with tyrannical arrogance, Alfred was a king who had touched the raw bottom of earthly misery.

54
00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:51.440
[George] Living among destitute peasants and learning that the sovereign was merely the servant of his people and his god.

55
00:07:51.440 --> 00:08:02.480
[George] The folklore of the burnt cakes immortalised the truth that true nobility is measured not by crowns and robes, but by humility, patience, and the willingness to learn from failure.

56
00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:16.560
[George] Sending secret messengers through the forests of Somerset, Wiltshire, and Hampshire, Alfred summoned the Fead, the militia of Saxon Freemen, to rally at Egbert's stone on the edge of Selwood Forest in early May 878.

57
00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:21.040
[George] When the warriors saw their lost king alive, they cheered with joy.

58
00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:26.400
[George] Leading his reformed army to Eddington, Alfred confronted Guthrum's Danish host.

59
00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:41.760
[George] Forming an impenetrable, interlocking shield wall, the Saxons absorbed the ferocious Viking assault, holding their ground before surging forward in a disciplined counterthrust that broke the Danish lines and rooted them back to their fortified camp at Chippenham.

60
00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:46.240
[George] After a fourteen-day siege, Guthrum surrendered unconditionally.

61
00:08:46.240 --> 00:09:01.320
[George] In the historic Treaty of Wedmore, Guthrum accepted Christian baptism with Alfred's standing as his godfather, agreeing to withdraw from Wessex and establishing the Danelaw, a legal boundary dividing England along Wattling Street from London to Chester.

62
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:07.160
[George] The muster at Egbert's Stone in May 878 was a triumph of underground logistics.

63
00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:17.320
[George] Over 4,000 Saxon warriors arrived with round wooden shields, spears and scramasax daggers, emerging silently from the ancient woodlands of Selwood.

64
00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:31.240
[George] At Eddington, Alfred deployed the classic Anglo-Saxon shield wall, the boardwheel, locking limewood shields with overlapping rims and thrusting ash spears through the gaps to repel Guthrum's repeated cavalry and axe charges.

65
00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:39.080
[George] When the Danish shield wall finally fractured under the relentless pressure of the Saxon counterattack, Alfred's victory was total.

66
00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:53.480
[George] At Aular near Athelny, Guthrum was submerged in the baptismal font, taking the Christian name Athelstan, with King Alfred embracing his former mortal enemy as a spiritual son in an extraordinary act of Christian statesmanship.

67
00:09:53.480 --> 00:10:02.760
[George] Recognizing that peace was merely an armed truce, Alfred undertook a revolutionary transformation of English military architecture and governance.

68
00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:18.520
[George] He constructed a nationwide network of thirty-three fortified towns, known as Burrs, including Winchester, Oxford, and Chichester, engineered so that no Saxon farmer was more than twenty miles, or a single day's march, from a fortified refuge.

69
00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:26.440
[George] He divided the Fiat into two rotating shifts, ensuring that half the men farmed the fields while the other half manned the garrisons.

70
00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:41.720
[George] To counter Viking sea raids, Alfred commissioned the construction of massive, sixty ored warships of his own design, faster, steadier, and higher out of the water than Danish dragonships, laying the embryonic foundation of the Royal Navy.

71
00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:51.840
[George] Finally, he codified the Dombach, or Book of Laws, harmonizing the legal codes of Ani, Offa, and Ethelbert under the divine framework of the Ten Commandments.

72
00:10:52.080 --> 00:11:05.920
[George] Alfred's fortified bar system was an administrative masterpiece documented in the Burgel Hitage, which mathematically calculated the exact number of garrison troops required to defend every pole of rampart walls across Wessex.

73
00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:21.120
[George] By rebuilding the Roman stone walls of London in 886 and placing it under the governance of his daughter Ethelfled and son-in-law Ethelred of Mercia, Alfred cemented the political union of Angles and Saxons into an embryonic English realm.

74
00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:37.760
[George] His innovative warships, measuring over sixty oars and constructed higher out of the water than Danish vessels, were specifically engineered to board enemy longships from above, sweeping their decks with archers and preventing Viking fleets from landing undetected on southern shores.

75
00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:42.960
[George] To Alfred, military security was meaningless without spiritual enlightenment.

76
00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:58.000
[George] Lamenting that Danish plunder had burned monastic libraries and left few south of the Humber able to read Latin, Alfred recruited world-class scholars, including Asser of Wales and Grimbald of Flanders, establishing a royal palace school.

77
00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:14.080
[George] Remarkably, in his late thirties, the warrior king taught himself Latin so he could personally translate essential texts into old English, including Gregory the Great's pastoral care, Bothius's consolation of philosophy, and Bede's ecclesiastical history.

78
00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:23.680
[George] He commissioned the Anglo Saxon Chronicle, the monumental annual record of English history that bound the disparate tribes into a single national consciousness.

79
00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:37.560
[George] Passing away in October 899 at age 50, King Alfred the Great left behind a unified, literate, and fortified Wessex, having saved the English language, culture, and monarchy from utter extinction.

80
00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:47.880
[George] Alfred recognized with tragic clarity that if the English language and Christian civilization were to survive, learning must be restored to the common people in their native tongue.

81
00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:57.080
[George] He established an educational decree mandating that all freeborn youths who possessed the means should be set to learning until they could read English writing with ease.

82
00:12:57.080 --> 00:13:10.280
[George] Translating Latin texts by the flickering light of horn lanterns that he had designed himself to shield candles from palace drafts, Alfred wrote touching personal prefaces, explaining that wisdom was the highest gift of God.

83
00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:23.320
[George] Alfred the Great was that rarest of historical figures, a brilliant battlefield general who was also an enlightened scholar, a devout Christian lawgiver, and the solitary father of the English nation.

84
00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:26.840
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

85
00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:35.080
[Rebecca] King Alfred the Great's scholar-warrior brilliance, Christian humility, and military genius created the foundation of the English nation.

86
00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:44.120
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with Alfred's grandson King Athelstan, who unified all the kingdoms of Britain at the epic Battle of Brunnenbar.

87
00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:47.400
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

